# Line endings.
#
# Deliberately NOT a blanket `* text=auto eol=lf`: the ~2600 .ts sources are left to
# whatever core.autocrlf the checkout has, exactly as before. Pinned here is only the
# small set (~100 files) where CRLF is actively harmful.

# Shell scripts run on Linux/macOS — server/deploy/*.sh on the VPS most of all. A CRLF
# shebang makes the kernel look for an interpreter literally named "bash\r".
# tools/gimp-export-layers/.gitattributes has carried this rule locally for a while;
# this lifts it to the root so scripts added elsewhere are covered too.
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.command   text eol=lf

# Node scripts, most of which carry a `#!/usr/bin/env node` shebang.
#
# CRLF there breaks more than direct execution: esbuild (and therefore vitest) emits
# invalid JS when it strips a shebang that ends in \r, so ANY test importing such a file
# dies during collection with `SyntaxError: Invalid or unexpected token` — reported
# against the importing TEST file, which points nowhere near the real culprit.
#
# Found 2026-09-12: server/commercial's suite failed in every freshly created worktree
# (checked out CRLF under core.autocrlf=true) while the main checkout, which happened to
# hold LF, stayed green — so it read as "my change broke a test" rather than as an
# environment artifact. See claudedocs/worktrees.md.
*.mjs       text eol=lf
*.cjs       text eol=lf
*.js        text eol=lf

# Windows-only, and cmd.exe wants CRLF.
*.cmd       text eol=crlf
